#!/usr/bin/python3
"""hearth-briefcase: the Briefcase in Hearth Files (FileManager.qml, Briefcase.qml), the Windows 95-XP way, made new.

A Briefcase is a folder with a small hidden list inside (.briefcase.json), so it travels with the folder: to a USB
stick, a shared folder or another Hearth device. Each item in it remembers where it came from (its original): a path on
this PC, a removable drive (its filesystem UUID and the path on it, so it's found under any mount point) or a shared
folder (smb://, opened through gio/gvfs), with the size, time and content hash of both copies at the last update.

  hearth-briefcase new PARENT [NAME] [--ensure]   make a Briefcase (--ensure: reuse PARENT/NAME if it's already one;
                                                Files never makes ~/Briefcase by itself)
  hearth-briefcase list                           the Briefcases this device knows (for the navigation pane and menus)
  hearth-briefcase status BC                      each item's status (and the lock's), recomputed now
  hearth-briefcase add BC SRC...                  copy SRC in and remember it as the original      } "progress: {json}"
  hearth-briefcase plan BC [NAME...]              what Update All / Update would do, row by row     } lines while
  hearth-briefcase apply BC PLAN_JSON             do the rows (each row's action as chosen)          } copying, then
  hearth-briefcase send BC DEST_FOLDER            copy the whole Briefcase (its list too) to DEST    } the final JSON
  hearth-briefcase transfer SRC_BC DEST_BC NAME... --copy|--move    an item to another Briefcase, keeping its link
  hearth-briefcase swap BC_A NAME_A BC_B NAME_B   exchange two items between two Briefcases
  hearth-briefcase split BC NAME...               make the copies independent (Split from original)
  hearth-briefcase find BC NAME                   where the original is (Find original)
  hearth-briefcase customize BC JSON              name, colour, icon, badge, note, view, sort, auto-lock, navigation pane
  hearth-briefcase reset-list BC                  start a damaged list again (the damaged one is kept aside)
  hearth-briefcase touch PATH                     Files opened something in an unlocked Briefcase (auto-lock timer)
Locking (gocryptfs; the password is read from stdin, never from the command line, and never written anywhere):
  hearth-briefcase lock-setup BC [--remember]     stdin: the password. Encrypt, verify, then the plain copy to Trash
  hearth-briefcase unlock BC [--remember|--keyring]   stdin: the password (--keyring: the one remembered)
  hearth-briefcase lock BC                        close it again
  hearth-briefcase lock-remove BC                 stdin: nothing (it must be unlocked). Decrypt, verify, then the
                                                  encrypted copy to Trash
  hearth-briefcase passwd BC                      stdin: old password, new password (one line each)
  hearth-briefcase forget BC                      forget the remembered password
  hearth-briefcase lock-all [--under PATH] [--reason sleep|eject|signout]
  hearth-briefcase guard                          (started by unlock) locks on sleep, sign-out and when idle
Auto-sync (hearth-briefcase-sync.service, a user service started after sign-in only when a Briefcase wants it):
  hearth-briefcase autosync                       the service: one-sided changes copied a few seconds after saving
  hearth-briefcase autosync-needed                exit 0 when at least one Briefcase has auto-sync on (ExecCondition)
  hearth-briefcase sync-now BC                    one automatic sync now (what the service does)

Rules: never lose a file. Copies go to a temporary file and are renamed over (atomic); a version that's replaced or
deleted goes to the Trash on its own side (the freedesktop Trash spec: your Trash, or the drive's .Trash-UID), or, on a
shared folder where there's no Trash, to .briefcase-replaced/<date> in the Briefcase. A file changed in both places is
never decided for you. The list is written with hearth_settings_io (atomic, .bak, a damaged one kept aside).
Python standard library only.
"""
